366 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 179 0 R
/T (Acknowledged Challenges & Opportunities:)
/E (Acknowledged Challenges & Opportunities:)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
367 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 755 0 R
/E (Interpretability is reduced. Compressing reasoning into continuous latent states means we lose the ability to read and audit the model's logic step-by-step. We trade transparency for efficiency )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
368 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 756 0 R
/E (Information bottleneck. The compressed latent states must preserve enough detail \(numbers, entities, logical relationships\) for the downstream language model to produce a correct answer. If the encoder's mean-pooling or the predictor's dimensionality is too aggressive, critical information is lost silently.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
369 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 757 0 R
/E (Parameter overhead vs. KV-cache savings. The JEPA module adds parameters and compute that wouldn't exist in a standard CoT pipeline. The net win depends on whether the reduced KV-cache pressure in the primary LLM outweighs the cost of running the predictor )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
370 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 758 0 R
/E (Step segmentation is a source of bias. Breaking a continuous CoT into discrete reasoning steps requires a heuristic \(e.g., paragraph splits\). Poor segmentation produces misaligned targets, which can bias the predictor toward learning artifacts of the splitting strategy rather than genuine logical transitions.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
